<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hypaspists</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The hypaspists were an elite infantry corps in the Macedonian army of Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. They fought alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> and were used for demanding tasks. According to the site, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>, served as their commander under Alexander.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hypaspists.jpg" alt="Hypaspists" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Macedonian_Army_Pezetairos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Greek word hypaspistes means shield bearer. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>'s Iliad Deiphobos advances under cover of his shield, and by the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> the word had come to mean a soldier of high status. A hypaspist differed from a skeuophoros, a baggage carrier, because the shield bearer was a free warrior and the baggage carrier a servant. The word may have had Homeric and heroic connotations that led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> to use it for an elite military unit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Macedonian hypaspists were probably armed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplite</a> manner, with a large concave shield and a spear, a body armour of spolas or linothorax, a helmet, greaves and a sword, though their equipment was likely more ornate than that of ordinary soldiers. The main Macedonian phalanx instead consisted of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pike">pikemen</a> called phalangites, armed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissa</a>, a pike of between 4 and 6.7 metres, and a small flat shield.</p><p>In set-piece battles the hypaspists stood on the flanks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a>, and light infantry and cavalry protected their own flanks. Their task was to guard the flanks of the large and unwieldy pike phalanx, since the phalangites were not agile or quick to turn. The phalanx served as the anvil, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/companion-cavalry">Companion cavalry</a> as the hammer, in the tactic of Philip II. The hypaspists could carry out manoeuvres that phalangites could not. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>'s phrase describing them has often been rendered 'lightest armed', although it is more properly translated as 'nimblest' or 'most agile'. They may have been raised from the whole kingdom rather than by canton, in which case they were the king's army rather than the army of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>All references to a unit called Hypaspists are much later than the time of Philip, and modern historians must assume that later writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a> had access to earlier records. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> hypaspists apparently continued under different names, such as the agema, in the Seleucid, Ptolemaic and Antigonid kingdoms, as royal bodyguards, elite infantry and military administrators. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> mentions a hypaspist sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-macedon">Philip V of Macedon</a> to Larissa to burn state papers after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-cynoscephalae">Battle of Cynoscephalae</a> in 197 BCE. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> the fighting unit seems to have lived on as the Antigonid peltasts, originally 3,000 men and 5,000 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hypaspists” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seleucus I Nicator, who had commanded the royal <b>hypaspists</b> under Alexander and the Companion cavalry under the regent Perdiccas, was appointed satrap of Babylonia in 320 BCE but was driven out by the dominant Diadochos Antigonus I Monophthalmus in 316 BCE, and spent several years in exile at…</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
